    "reason": "convertBeckettChecklistXlsx.cjs's explicitAnchor bypass (see CF-BECKETT-EXPLICIT-ANCHOR-IS-NOT-A-BLANK-CHEQUE in the script) still misfires on this workbook: a Memorabilia-sheet section whose OWN name contains a FINISH_WORD token ('Prizm') clears looksLikeFinishName and is tested for a numeric-only fold onto the Base/Concourse explicitAnchor, bypassing extendsName -- with no roster check in that pass. Four already-registered named insert products fold onto Base>Base Concourse as fabricated parallel names purely because their own numbering (1-25/1-42) is a 100% subset of Concourse's 1-100 range, even though their rosters DISAGREE with Concourse card-for-card (measured: Base Concourse #1 = Tory Taylor; the four sections' own #1 = Caleb Williams / Adonai Mitchell / Caleb Williams / Kurt Warner -- verified via rosterFoldAgainst, which correctly reports disagree>0 for this pairing, proving the roster-fold pass is NOT the one firing here): insert-draft-selections-memorabilia-prizm (25 rows, -> registered panini-select-draft-selections-memorabilia), insert-jumbo-rookie-swatch-prizm (42 rows, -> registered panini-select-jumbo-rookie-swatch), insert-rookie-swatches-prizm (25 rows, -> registered panini-select-rookie-swatches), insert-sparks-prizm (58 rows, -> registered panini-select-sparks). 150 of 18,886 rows (0.8%) land under category=base with a fabricated parallel string instead of their own registered insert-set key. Every other row in this file (18,736) is unaffected -- base anchors, autograph sections (isAuto true/false populated on every row, no blanks), and every other insert/parallel fold were spot-checked clean. Held whole-file rather than partially, because the ingester's manifest gate only supports an all-or-nothing hold and 'absent beats wrong' for the 150 known-bad rows outweighs shipping the 18,736 good ones early. Unhold once the converter's explicitAnchor+FINISH_WORD bypass gets a roster check (or an equivalent fix) and the four sections re-emit under their own registered category with a blank parallel."
